# 6. Technology Comparison

| Technology | Main purpose | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| Logistic Regression | Baseline classification | Employee features | Attrition prediction |
| Decision Tree | Interpretable classification | Employee features | Prediction + decision rules |
| Random Forest | Robust tabular prediction | Employee features | Attrition prediction + feature importance |
| Gradient Boosting | Strong tabular prediction | Employee features | Attrition prediction |
| SHAP | Explain ML predictions | Model + features | Feature contributions |
| LLM | Natural-language interaction | Question + context | Natural-language answer |
| Embeddings | Semantic representation | Text | Vectors |
| Vector Database | Similarity retrieval | Vectors | Relevant document chunks |
| RAG | Ground LLM with external knowledge | Question + documents | Evidence-grounded context |
| Agentic Workflow | Coordinate tools | User goal | Multi-step result |
| Dashboard | Decision support | Analytics + AI outputs | Interactive insights |

## Why combine them?

**SQL** provides reliable structured workforce analytics.

**Python + ML** provides attrition prediction.

**SHAP** helps explain predictions.

**Embeddings + vector search** retrieve relevant document content.

**RAG** provides organization-specific knowledge to the LLM.

**LLM** provides natural-language interaction and summarization.

**Agents** coordinate the different tools.

**Dashboard** presents the resulting intelligence to HR users.

The value comes from integrating these components rather than claiming that an individual algorithm is completely new.
